[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch0
diagnosis_text:
The failure is in Campus 1’s distribution L3 switch mswitch0, specifically its routing/OSPF subsystem (FRR). Campus 1 hosts (VLANs 10/20/30) can reach their default gateway and each other because those subnets are directly connected on mswitch0 (172.16.10.254/20.254/30.254). However, traffic to Campus 2 (10.5.0.0/24, 10.6.0.0/24) and the backbone fails because mswitch0 has no upstream routes and returns ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable.”

Evidence:
- From pc5 to 10.6.0.4, the gateway 172.16.10.254 responds “Destination Net Unreachable,” proving the L3 gateway is dropping non-local traffic.
- On mswitch0, the routing table contains only connected routes (10.2.2.0/24, 172.16.10/20/30) with no default or learned routes.
- FRR on mswitch0 shows Active: failed, and its running configuration is effectively empty (no “router ospf” or redistribution), so there are no OSPF neighbors or advertisements.
- Upstream routers (router16 and router11) are healthy and have OSPF routes, but they do not have the 172.16.10/20/30 prefixes—consistent with mswitch0 not advertising them, which explains why router11 “stopped showing the Campus 1 prefixes.”

Root cause: mswitch0 lost its FRR/OSPF configuration (and thus upstream routes), causing Campus 1 to be isolated from the backbone and Campus 2.

Corrective fix: Restore the FRR configuration on mswitch0 (enable OSPF with router16 over 10.2.2.0/24 and advertise 172.16.10/20/30), then restart FRR. As a temporary workaround, add a static default route on mswitch0 to 10.2.2.1. Verify router11 learns the 172.16.x routes and end-to-end reachability returns. === END ===